{
  "requirement": "A parse result MUST retain the source snapshot it describes. Cached parsing\nMUST NOT be paired with bytes from a different source snapshot.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Obligations**\n1. A parse result MUST retain the source snapshot it describes.\n2. Cached parsing MUST NOT be paired with bytes from a different source snapshot.\n\n**Mapping to tests**\n- **Obligation 1:**\n  - `tests/sources.rs#fn[defaults_use_provider_primitives_and_keep_one_parse_snapshot]` parses through the default path. The `Memory` provider's read counter is 1 afterwards, so the parse used a single read.\n  - It asserts that `parsed.source.text()` starts with \"version 0\".\n  - It then reads the resource again and gets \"version 1\". It asserts that `parsed.source` is still \"version 0\".\n  - It also asserts that the outline's `node_lines` over the retained source contains \"version 0\". The outline therefore describes the same snapshot as the retained bytes.\n  - Its final assertion on the provider's recorded events (`[\"src/one.txt\", \"src/one.txt\", \"list\", \"src/one.txt\"]`) pins the exact sequence of reads.\n  - `tests/sources.rs#fn[list_read_only_provider_supports_selected_language_parse_and_structural_search]` asserts that `parsed.source.text()` equals the provider text and that the outline resolves `fn[provided]`. This covers the explicit-language case and the extension-inferred case.\n- **Obligation 2:**\n  - `tests/agent.rs#fn[cached_selected_source_reads_record_exact_delivered_contents_and_errors_add_no_input]` uses `CachedProvider`. Its `read` always errors with \"must not refetch\". Its `parse` builds the result with `parse_snapshot` from fixed bytes.\n  - The graph's current source is deliberately different (\"different current source\").\n  - The tool flow runs `search_structure`, `outline_source` and `read_source` with `fn[observed]` and `fn[missing]`.\n  - The final assertion requires the recorded dependency fingerprint to equal exactly \"fn observed() { call(); }\" under the \"selected-content\" scheme. That value is taken from the retained `parsed.source` and its outline.\n  - A violation would show up as a refetch error or as bytes from the graph's current source (\"different current source\").\n\n**Gaps**\nI found none. Both clauses have a test with assertions that would fail on a violation. The tests are marked with `{test}` references to the subject. The implementation site is `sources.rs#struct[ParsedSource]`, which carries `source` and `outline` together. The default `parse` and `parse_sync` both build their results through `parse_snapshot` on bytes from one read.",
  "references": "213dc0bd7a8b4861107c95e8adf98f9ff886f19bdbdaf3e26c8a3af2a3ff58d1",
